---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CronExpressionParser from '@/components/tools/dev/CronExpressionParser.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="cron-expression-parser">
  <CronExpressionParser client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to read and build a cron expression</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type an expression, or start from a preset and adjust it.</li>
      <li>Each field also has its own box, so you can change only the hour without counting positions.</li>
      <li>The plain-English sentence updates as you type, and each error names the wrong field and the reason.</li>
      <li>Check the next ten run times before you deploy the schedule. The description and the real behaviour can differ in ways that are easy to miss.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The fields</h2>
    <p>
      Standard crontab uses five fields. Quartz, the Spring scheduler and node-cron accept six, with
      seconds first. This cron expression parser detects the format from the number of fields.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Position (5-field)</th><th>Field</th><th>Range</th><th>Names accepted</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>n/a</td><td>Second (6-field only)</td><td>0-59</td><td>None</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>Minute</td><td>0-59</td><td>None</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>Hour</td><td>0-23</td><td>None</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>Day of month</td><td>1-31</td><td>None</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>Month</td><td>1-12</td><td>JAN-DEC</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td>Day of week</td><td>0-7</td><td>SUN-SAT; both 0 and 7 mean Sunday</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Syntax within a field</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Syntax</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>*</code></td><td>Every value</td><td><code>* * * * *</code>: every minute</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>5</code></td><td>One value</td><td><code>5 * * * *</code>: five past every hour</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>1,15,30</code></td><td>A list</td><td><code>0 1,13 * * *</code>: 01:00 and 13:00</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>1-5</code></td><td>An inclusive range</td><td><code>0 9 * * 1-5</code>: 09:00 on weekdays</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>*/15</code></td><td>A step from the lowest value</td><td><code>*/15 * * * *</code>: :00, :15, :30, :45</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>10-40/5</code></td><td>A step inside a range</td><td>10, 15, 20 … 40</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>?</code></td><td>Treated as <code>*</code> in the two day fields</td><td>Quartz convention</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h2>The day-of-month and day-of-week trap</h2>
    <p>
      Almost everyone gets this rule wrong once. When both the day-of-month and the day-of-week field are
      restricted, classic Vixie cron runs the job when <strong>either</strong> matches, not when both
      match. So <code>0 0 1 * MON</code> runs on the first of each month and also on each Monday: about
      five times a month, not once.
    </p>
    <p>
      For an "and", restrict only one field in cron and check the other in your job:
      <code>0 0 1-7 * *</code> plus an "is it Monday?" test at the top of the script gives "the first
      Monday of the month".
    </p>

    <h2>Shortcut keywords</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Shortcut</th><th>Equivalent</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>@yearly</code>, <code>@annually</code></td><td><code>0 0 1 1 *</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@monthly</code></td><td><code>0 0 1 * *</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@weekly</code></td><td><code>0 0 * * 0</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@daily</code>, <code>@midnight</code></td><td><code>0 0 * * *</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@hourly</code></td><td><code>0 * * * *</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Steps do not mean "every N from now"</h2>
    <p>
      <code>*/20</code> in the hour field means hours 0 and 20, then it stops, because 40 is out of
      range. It does not mean "every 20 hours". A step that does not divide the field range evenly gives
      an uneven gap at the wrap-around: <code>*/45</code> in the minute field runs at :00 and :45, then
      15 minutes later at :00. For a regular interval that does not divide 60, use a scheduler with
      interval support.
    </p>

    <h2>Timezones and DST</h2>
    <p>
      The run times here are calculated in the timezone of your device, shown above the table. A cron
      daemon uses the system timezone, or the value of <code>CRON_TZ</code> if the crontab sets one.
      Container images are often UTC, which usually explains a job that runs "an hour early".
    </p>
    <p>
      Take care with jobs scheduled between 01:00 and 03:00 local time: on the spring-forward day that
      hour does not exist, and on the autumn day it happens twice. Cron implementations handle this in
      different ways. Schedule such jobs in UTC to avoid the problem.
    </p>

    <h2>What this parser does not support</h2>
    <p>
      The Quartz extensions <code>L</code> (last), <code>W</code> (nearest weekday) and <code>#</code>
      (nth weekday of the month) are rejected with a clear message, because their meaning differs
      between implementations. The full POSIX and Vixie grammar is supported.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
